---
import Guide from '../layouts/Guide.astro';
import SecHead from '../components/SecHead.astro';
import Tag from '../components/Tag.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const u = (p: string) => `${base}${p}`;
const faq = [
  { q: 'What is an FPGA AI accelerator?', a: 'An FPGA AI accelerator is neural-network hardware built from an FPGA\'s logic, multipliers and memory. Instead of running instructions, the network\'s multiply-adds are wired into the chip, so many neurons compute in the same clock cycle. NetSilcon is an open-source example with two engines on a Sipeed Tang Nano 20K.' },
  { q: 'Is an FPGA AI accelerator faster than a CPU?', a: 'Per clock cycle, by a wide margin: NetSilcon-64x6 needs 448 cycles per inference where a laptop CPU needs about 14,100 for the same bit-exact network. In wall-clock time a 4 GHz CPU can still win against a 27 MHz FPGA. The FPGA wins on cycles, on fixed latency and on energy, and it beats the microcontroller that would normally sit in an embedded device.' },
  { q: 'Can I change the AI model without rebuilding the FPGA?', a: 'Yes, if the weights live in writable registers or block RAM. Both NetSilcon engines load a new model over UART: 201 bytes in 31 ms for NetSilcon-6x4, 23,335 bytes in 2 s for NetSilcon-64x6. A CRC check guards every upload, and the running model is only replaced after the check passes.' },
  { q: 'Which FPGA is good for a small AI accelerator?', a: 'For sensor-level networks a low-cost FPGA is enough. NetSilcon uses the Gowin GW2AR-18 on the Sipeed Tang Nano 20K: NetSilcon-64x6 needs 35 % of its logic, 32 of 46 block RAMs and 16 of 24 DSP blocks. The number of hardware multipliers and block RAMs usually decides how big a network fits.' },
  { q: 'FPGA vs NPU for edge AI: which is better?', a: 'An NPU is best for large models like vision networks, where it runs at full speed. For tiny networks with batch size 1, the overhead of moving data to an NPU can cost more than the inference itself. An FPGA accelerator answers in a fixed number of clock cycles with no driver or operating system in the way.' },
];
---
<Guide
  path="/fpga-ai-accelerator/"
  title="FPGA AI accelerator: an open-source design for edge inference."
  fullTitle="FPGA AI accelerator for edge inference: open-source int8 design · NetSilcon"
  description="An open-source FPGA AI accelerator for edge inference: two int8 neural network engines with loadable weights, measured on a Tang Nano 20K. Architecture, numbers, trade-offs."
  lede="An FPGA AI accelerator puts a neural network's arithmetic straight into hardware. This guide explains the two common ways to build one, when an FPGA beats a CPU, a microcontroller or an NPU, and what the numbers look like on two real open-source engines that run today."
  updated="2026-11-22"
  about={['FPGA AI accelerator', 'Edge AI', 'Neural network on FPGA', 'int8 inference']}
  toc={[{ id: 'what', label: 'What it is' }, { id: 'arch', label: 'Two architectures' }, { id: 'numbers', label: 'Measured numbers' }, { id: 'when', label: 'When an FPGA wins' }, { id: 'load', label: 'Loadable models' }]}
  faq={faq}
  related={[{ href: '/neural-network-on-fpga/', label: 'Neural network on FPGA: the full guide' }, { href: '/verilog-neural-network/', label: 'Neural network in Verilog: MAC, ReLU, requantization' }, { href: '/netsilcon-64x6/', label: 'NetSilcon-64x6: 22,528 weights, 448 clocks' }, { href: '/benchmarks/', label: 'Benchmarks: FPGA vs MCU, CPU, GPU, NPU' }]}
>
  <section class="sec" aria-labelledby="what">
    <SecHead no="§1" title="What an FPGA AI accelerator is." id="what" />
    <div class="prose guide-prose">
      <p>A neural network is mostly multiply-and-add: every neuron multiplies its inputs by weights, adds them up, adds a bias and passes the result through an activation such as ReLU. A processor does those steps one instruction at a time. An <b>FPGA AI accelerator</b> builds the multipliers and adders as real circuits, so dozens of neurons compute in the same clock cycle and the timing is the same on every run.</p>
      <p>At the edge, the job is usually small and urgent: a few sensor readings in, one decision out, right now. That is exactly where a compact accelerator pays off. It needs no operating system, no driver and no batch of inputs to be efficient.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec" aria-labelledby="arch">
    <SecHead no="§2" title="Two ways to build one: fully parallel or lane-based." id="arch">The two NetSilcon engines show both ends of the design space on the same board.</SecHead>
    <div class="cols cols-2">
      <div>
        <span class="step-no">fully parallel · NetSilcon-6x4</span>
        <h3>Every neuron has its own multipliers</h3>
        <p class="mut">A 4 → 6 → 6 → 6 → 6 → 2 network with 144 weights in registers and 72 multipliers shared over two phases. One answer every 15 clock cycles (0.556 µs at 27 MHz) <Tag b="M" />. Ideal when the network is tiny and latency is everything.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <span class="step-no">lane-based · NetSilcon-64x6</span>
        <h3>64 lanes, weights streamed from block RAM</h3>
        <p class="mut">A 16 → 64×6 → 16 network with 22,528 weights in 32 block RAMs. One input value is broadcast to 64 multiply-accumulate lanes per clock; a shared requantizer finishes each layer. 448 clock cycles per inference <Tag b="M" />. This scales to far bigger networks on a small FPGA.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec" aria-labelledby="numbers">
    <SecHead no="§3" title="Measured numbers, not marketing." id="numbers">Both engines run on a Sipeed Tang Nano 20K (Gowin GW2AR-18) at 27 MHz. Every hardware answer was checked bit for bit against a Python int8 model.</SecHead>
    <div class="tbl-wrap guide-tbl">
      <table class="stack">
        <thead><tr><th></th><th>NetSilcon-6x4</th><th>NetSilcon-64x6</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row" data-l="">Weights</th><td data-l="6x4">144</td><td data-l="64x6">22,528</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row" data-l="">Latency per inference</th><td data-l="6x4">15 clocks · 0.556 µs <Tag b="M" /></td><td data-l="64x6">448 clocks · 16.6 µs <Tag b="M" /></td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row" data-l="">Bit-exact test vectors on the FPGA</th><td data-l="6x4">576 per application</td><td data-l="64x6">3,200</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row" data-l="">Hardware-in-the-loop decisions</th><td data-l="6x4">9,600, 0 mismatches</td><td data-l="64x6">19,200, 0 mismatches</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row" data-l="">FPGA resources</th><td data-l="6x4">49 % logic, 24 / 24 DSP</td><td data-l="64x6">35 % logic, 32 / 46 BSRAM, 16 / 24 DSP</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row" data-l="">Compared with</th><td data-l="6x4">≈ 25× faster than an STM32F407 (estimate)</td><td data-l="64x6">31× fewer clock cycles than a laptop CPU (measured)</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec" aria-labelledby="when">
    <SecHead no="§4" title="When an FPGA accelerator is the right choice." id="when" />
    <div class="prose guide-prose">
      <p><b>Choose an FPGA</b> when the network is small to medium, inputs arrive one at a time (batch size 1), the answer must come in a fixed time, and power or cost rules out a big processor. Motor-fault detection beside an ESC, battery-cell anomaly checks and obstacle decisions on a robot all fit this pattern.</p>
      <p><b>Choose a CPU</b> when you already have a fast one and only need an answer now and then. At 4 GHz a laptop core finishes NetSilcon-64x6's network in 3.45 µs; the FPGA engine would need about 130 MHz to match it in wall-clock time, even though it needs 31× fewer cycles.</p>
      <p><b>Choose an NPU or GPU</b> for vision, audio and language models, where their throughput dominates and batch sizes are large.</p>
      <p>The cycle count is what carries over to a custom chip. A design that needs 448 cycles, clocked at a few hundred MHz in silicon, would beat the laptop at a tiny fraction of the power. That is the long-term path NetSilcon is built for.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec" aria-labelledby="load">
    <SecHead no="§5" title="Loadable models: one accelerator, many jobs." id="load" />
    <div class="prose guide-prose">
      <p>Most FPGA accelerators bake the weights into the bitstream, so a new model means a new synthesis run. NetSilcon fixes only the <b>shape</b> in hardware and keeps the <b>numbers</b> loadable. The same NetSilcon-6x4 bitstream has been a motor-fault detector, an irrigation controller and a rover driver, just by uploading a different file.</p>
      <p>Safety comes first: an upload goes into a staging buffer (6x4) or the inactive weight bank (64x6), is checked with a CRC, and is swapped in only if the check passes. A damaged upload is refused and the running model keeps working. <a href={u('/docs/model-image/')}>How the model image is laid out →</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Guide>
